      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film represents a crucial pivot in Truffaut's career, marking his deeper engagement with Hitchcockian themes after publishing his famous interview book \"Hitchcock/Truffaut\" in 1966. Like <a href=\"/movie/426\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"426\">Vertigo</a> (1958), it explores how men can project fantasies onto mysterious women, though Truffaut inverts expectations by making Deneuve's character the active agent of deception. The noir elements recall American films like <a href=\"/movie/996\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"996\">Double Indemnity</a> (1944), but filtered through a distinctly French lens that's more interested in psychological complexity than plot mechanics."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Deneuve's performance builds on her ice-queen persona established in <a href=\"/movie/649\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"649\">Belle de Jour</a> (1967) and <a href=\"/movie/11481\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"11481\">Repulsion</a> (1965), but adds layers of vulnerability beneath the duplicity. The film's influence can be seen in later French thrillers like <a href=\"/movie/12531\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"12531\">L'Appartement</a> (1996) and even American neo-noirs such as <a href=\"/movie/25284\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"25284\">The Last Seduction</a> (1994), where femmes fatales drive the narrative rather than simply decorating it. SUBHEAD: The New Wave Noir Connection"
      },
      {
