      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "While often overlooked in discussions of French New Wave cinema, Mississippi Mermaid demonstrates how the movement could absorb and transform Hollywood genres. Like Godard's <a href=\"/movie/269\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"269\">Breathless</a> (1960) and <a href=\"/movie/8073\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8073\">Band of Outsiders</a> (1964), it takes American noir conventions but infuses them with psychological realism and formal experimentation. The film's long takes and natural lighting contrast sharply with classic noir style, creating a more immediate sense of emotional truth."
      }
